I'm Amber Straughn, an astrophysicist at NASA.&nbsp;
And today we're going to be building a model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:33.534
of the James Webb Space Telescope, the largest,&nbsp;
most complex, most powerful telescope NASA has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:35.908
ever sent to space.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.040
This telescope has been in development since&nbsp;
1996. Bill Clinton was president. I looked it up;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.000
The top song on the radio was the Macarena.&nbsp;
And that wasn't too far after the Hubble went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.360
up into orbit.&nbsp;
Right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:07.520
So what was inspiring the folks at NASA at the&nbsp;
time to start planning for another one so soon?&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.320
We've been able to see very distant galaxies with&nbsp;
Hubble. But if you look in these deep fields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.920
you sort of see where you come up to the&nbsp;
edge, where there are no more galaxies. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.720
we know there are more galaxies.&nbsp;
We knew that in order to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.920
extremely distant galaxies, we would need an&nbsp;
infrared telescope. And J.W.S.T was specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.640
designed to be able to see the first epoch of&nbsp;
galaxies that were born after the Big Bang.&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.160
How does that work?

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:42.720
Yeah. So we're basically able to do that because&nbsp;
light takes time to travel across the universe. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.920
you can sort of think of you know the light from&nbsp;
the sun takes about 8 minutes to get to the Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.160
So we’re seeing the sun as it was 8 minutes&nbsp;
ago. If the sun went out right now, wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.840
know it for 8 minutes. And then galaxies that are&nbsp;
further away, the light from them takes longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.280
to get here. So we're seeing them as they were&nbsp;
further in the past, but they very likely now have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.040
stars and planets and evolved possibly, you know,&nbsp;
evolved life forms, which is super exciting.&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.160
Maybe even life forms that build telescopes?
Who knows, right? They could be they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.000
be looking back at us, but of course&nbsp;
they would be seeing us as we were.&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.920
Yeah, what would they see if they were doing&nbsp;
exactly the same thing that we're doing?&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.080
They would. I mean, they would basically&nbsp;
see the same thing that we're going to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.840
when we look at them.
Which is like primitive galaxies

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:35.040
Exactly. So basically, not long after the Big&nbsp;
Bang, the only thing in the universe was hydrogen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:41.040
and helium. But there were some parts of the&nbsp;
universe that were more dense than other parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.760
and that collapse of that hydrogen&nbsp;
and helium were the first stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.640
and the first galaxies.&nbsp;And so by, you know, by&nbsp;
looking at the different galaxies at different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.520
distances, we're sort of able to put together&nbsp;
a picture of how galaxies change over time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.880
Ok so Gonna pop this on. Slide this on this way.&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:10.240
So this is where all the electronics will be.&nbsp;
Here we have our solar array which is where we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:17.840
power. And this is a communications antenna so&nbsp;
we can talk to the spacecraft once it's in space.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.440
What are these?
The telescope, the mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.320
and the instruments have to be shielded from&nbsp;
the light and the heat of the sun. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.120
you think of a normal telescope like Hubble or&nbsp;
your backyard telescope and those telescopes have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:40.080
tubes, right? That's what shields those mirrors.&nbsp;
This telescope, since it's so big and also since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.480
it's going to be very far away in space, we don't&nbsp;
really need a tube. We only need a shield. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.680
mirrors and instruments will be able to stay very,&nbsp;
very cold. That is important because this is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:57.600
infrared telescope and we have to use infrared&nbsp;
light to see those first stars and galaxies but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.160
everything above absolute zero glows in infrared.&nbsp;
So you and I glow in infrared, the planet, the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.440
you know, everything is infrared.&nbsp;
That’s like thermal camera, night vision?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.440
Exactly. Yeah, you can sort of think of,&nbsp;
yeah, night vision goggles. If it was warm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.640
it would sort of glow and sort of see itself. So&nbsp;
that's why we're putting it out in deep space. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:22.720
that's why we need these amazing sunshields.
So where exactly in space will this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.560
telescope live?
So we are sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.720
this telescope about four times further away&nbsp;
than the Moon to give you a sense of scale.&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.400
OK, so Hubble is like really,&nbsp;
really close compared to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.680
and Hubble's going around us. So it's getting&nbsp;
closer to the sun farther away from the sun.&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:38.640
Right.
This thing is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.640
stay on our night side, on the Mars side of Earth&nbsp;
and just kind of head around the sun with us.&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.000
Yes.
But always on the outside.&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.280
Exactly. Yeah, it will sort of you can&nbsp;
think of it — It'll always be sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:56.000
in the midnight sky, sort of straight above.
So the sun shields are keeping the heat away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.640
from the mirror and the instruments. How&nbsp;
hot would it get if they weren't there?&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.320
So the sun is always shining on this bottom&nbsp;
side of the telescope and this is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:15.440
two hundred degrees Fahrenheit. And the cold&nbsp;
side will end up being about 400 degrees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.920
below zero Fahrenheit. So it&nbsp;
ends up being about a 600 degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.840
difference between the hot side and the cold side.
Just because of these five little super thin?&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.280
Yeah.
How does that work?&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:33.120
So the reason that there are these five&nbsp;
layers is because in between each layer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.800
the heat basically is radiated outward, OK? And&nbsp;
so by the time you get to each successive layer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.200
it's a little bit cooler. And so the that's&nbsp;
the reason one sunshield wouldn't work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:50.647
This design gives us a more — more efficient&nbsp;
way to get it very, very cold on the top.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.600
OK, so now we're going to build the&nbsp;
mirror here. So if you want to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

