glue dots on the back and then hand&nbsp;
me the piece? So just to get back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.120
the basics, why do telescopes have mirrors?
So mirrors are essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.760
light buckets. So the size of a mirror is really&nbsp;
important because if you think about sitting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.480
bucket outside when it's raining, the bigger your&nbsp;
bucket, the more water you're going to collect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:24.800
This mirror is about twenty one and a half feet&nbsp;
across. It's about six times the light collecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.240
area of Hubble. And in comparison to Hubble, of&nbsp;
course, Hubble is our gold standard of telescopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.440
and we love Hubble.
We do.&nbsp;

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.520
But when we take into account the size of&nbsp;
the mirror, the wavelength range that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.160
telescope will see in, the infrared light,&nbsp;
and the sort of efficiency of the detectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.320
we expect this telescope to be about&nbsp;
100 times more powerful than Hubble.&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.000
That’s crazy.
It’s gonna be awesome.&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.880
And so why gold and why hexagons?
So gold, it turns out, is a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:04.080
reflector of infrared light. The reason it's in&nbsp;
hexagons is we can't launch it fully deployed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.520
a full mirror. So we have to fold it and hexagons&nbsp;
pack together very nicely and are very foldable.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:20.240
So basically these three mirrors and then these&nbsp;
three mirrors will fold backwards.&nbsp;And then once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:26.000
it gets to space, it has to do the opposite.&nbsp;
It has to unfold. And then we have a telescope.&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.000
And how long does that process take?
That whole process takes about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.400
two weeks from start to finish, so it's&nbsp;
going to be a tense two weeks after launch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:39.440
And if something doesn't happen exactly as&nbsp;
it's supposed to or exactly as we've planned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.840
we can pause, figure out what's&nbsp;
going on and then work to fix it.&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:47.520
But fixing it does not include&nbsp;
sending an astronaut there to fix it.&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.640
Definitely not. So it's going to be too far away&nbsp;
to send astronauts to. You know, there is some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.440
risk involved in in building a telescope like&nbsp;
this. We can't go fix it. And that's one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.240
reasons it's taken so long to test it, to make&nbsp;
sure it's going to work like it's supposed to.

